# VoxCPM2 TTS server (cloned voice + per-utterance style notes) — SATURN, on the RTX 3090.
#
# The live voice once the Saturn move is done (Jupiter TTS_URL=http://10.0.0.58:8004). It moved off
# Helios GPU1 so the brain can run its native 262144 context (header of
# tts/llama-server-primary.service). Same server and API as before (tts/voxcpm_server.py); only the
# box changed. tts/voxcpm-tts.service is the Helios copy, kept installed but DISABLED as rollback.
#
# Which card: the RTX 3090 (24 GiB, Ampere sm_86; owner decision 2026-11-12). Saturn's 10 GiB
# RTX 3080 was the first pick and is too small: an empty 3080 shows 9.64 GiB free in nvidia-smi,
# and VoxCPM2 takes ~7.4 GiB fresh and grew 7.3 -> 9.3 GiB on Helios in one week of ordinary texts
# (all under 610 chars). PyTorch keeps memory it has used cached, and nothing in voxcpm releases
# it. The VAE decodes a whole clip at once in fp32, so the peak grows with text length. The 3080
# is now the owner's experiment card. A 16 GB RTX 5080 may take the voice later (it needs a new
# PSU): put its UUID in /etc/voxcpm-tts.env, restart, re-run the memory test in step 7, move the
# experiment pin to the card the voice leaves, and in a PR change TTSGPUMemoryLow
# (monitoring/prometheus/alert-rules.yml): the card name to 5080 and the used-memory line to what
# the test measured plus ~1 GiB (16 GiB can never be exceeded on a 16 GB card). A name that matches
# no card makes that alert silently never fire. The torch 2.8 cu128 wheels carry Ampere kernels as
# well as Blackwell, so the Helios package list installs unchanged.
# Don't add PYTORCH_CUDA_ALLOC_CONF=expandable_segments to save memory: VoxCPM2 compiles with
# mode="reduce-overhead" (CUDA graphs), and the two were never tested together.
# Experiments MUST be pinned to the 3080: llama.cpp auto-fit and most tools use every GPU they can
# see, and CUDA numbers the 3090 first (cuda:0, `--gpus all`) while nvidia-smi lists the 3080 first
# (index 0), so pin by UUID only, never by index. Put these two lines at the TOP of ~/.bashrc,
# above the line that returns early for non-interactive shells, so `ssh saturn 'cmd'` gets them:
#   export CUDA_DEVICE_ORDER=PCI_BUS_ID
#   export CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES=GPU-<3080 uuid>
# Check: `ssh labadmin@10.0.0.58 'echo $CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES'` prints the 3080's UUID. That doesn't
# reach Docker containers, cron jobs or systemd units: give those `--gpus device=GPU-<3080 uuid>`
# or their own CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES. An unpinned experiment shares the voice card: it slows
# synthesis, and one that fills the card makes synthesis fail while /health stays 200.
# TTSGPUMemoryLow (quiet, more than 16 GiB used on the 3090) and TTSSynthesisFailing (quiet, 5
# failures in 30 min) catch most of that; a co-tenant under ~6 GiB only shows as slower speech.
# Publish experiment servers on 127.0.0.1 (`docker run -p 127.0.0.1:PORT:PORT`), not on every
# interface: Saturn also holds the voice clip and the off-box backups.
# Saturn runs NVIDIA's OPEN kernel module (nvidia-580-open), which drives Ampere and Blackwell
# alike (a later 5080 needs it). Unattended upgrades can swap the driver under the loaded module
# (Helios holds its 580 packages for this reason: `apt-mark showhold`). SaturnGpuDriverBroken
# (quiet) catches it.
#
# What Jess's voice now depends on: Saturn being up. A Saturn outage pages as NodeDown; TTSDown
# (quiet) fires when Saturn is up but this server isn't answering. Saturn kernel bumps have dropped
# the NVIDIA module before (2026-11-07): if `nvidia-smi` fails after an upgrade, check
# /lib/modules/$(uname -r)/kernel/nvidia-580-open and install linux-modules-nvidia-580-open-$(uname -r).
# The server refuses to run on CPU (VOXCPM_REQUIRE_CUDA), so a lost driver shows up as TTSDown, not
# as a ~7 s/sentence voice.
#
# Install on Saturn (one-time, as labadmin). Everything the server runs comes from the working Helios
# install, checked by sha256, plus the pinned package list tts/requirements-voxcpm.txt; nothing is
# piped from the internet into a shell. Keep /home/labadmin at mode 750: the service never needs to
# traverse it (systemd bind-mounts the four dirs below into an otherwise empty /home).
#   0. `nvidia-smi -L` lists the RTX 3080 and the RTX 3090. Note both UUIDs (GPU-xxxxxxxx-...):
#      the 3090's goes in step 5, the 3080's in the experiment pin ("Experiments MUST be pinned"
#      above). Keep the real UUIDs out of the repo and the PR. Then clear the 3090:
#        docker ps -a --filter name=expert-model --filter name=vision-model
#      `expert-model` (the retired Qwen3-32B) is bound to the 3090 and publishes an unauthenticated
#      API on :8084 on every interface; started by anyone, it would put ~20 GiB on the voice card.
#      Remove it: `docker rm expert-model`. `vision-model` (retired, on the 3080, :8010 on every
#      interface) can't reach the voice card; remove it too unless you want it for experiments, and
#      then re-create it with `-p 127.0.0.1:8010:8080`. Finally `nvidia-smi` shows no process on the
#      3090.
#   1. Service user (no login, no home):  sudo useradd -r -M -s /usr/sbin/nologin voxcpm
#   2. Copy from Helios through Jupiter: the uv binary, uv's managed Python 3.11, the weights, and the
#      voice config with the one clip it uses. Then copy the server, package list and this unit from
#      git (the PR branch until it is merged, then main). git show reads a ref without touching the
#      live checkout's files. Run these ON JUPITER, in /home/labadmin/gateway_nerves (~5 GB):
#        ssh labadmin@10.0.0.195 'tar -C /home/labadmin -cf - .uv-pkg/bin/uv .local/share/uv/python models/VoxCPM2 tts-voices/voices.json tts-voices/jessica_sample.wav' \
#          | ssh labadmin@10.0.0.58 'tar -C /home/labadmin -xf -'
#        REF=origin/claude/upbeat-wescoff-4617d8; git fetch --no-write-fetch-head origin claude/upbeat-wescoff-4617d8
#        ssh labadmin@10.0.0.58 'mkdir -p voxcpm'
#        git show $REF:tts/voxcpm_server.py | ssh labadmin@10.0.0.58 'cat > voxcpm/voxcpm_server.py'
#        git show $REF:tts/requirements-voxcpm.txt | ssh labadmin@10.0.0.58 'cat > voxcpm/requirements-voxcpm.txt'
#        git show $REF:tts/voxcpm-tts-saturn.service | ssh labadmin@10.0.0.58 'cat > /tmp/voxcpm-tts-saturn.service'
#      then on Saturn check the copies (must print OK three times):
#        cd ~ && printf '%s\n' \
#          'db919fb9428cc8ad04377ba3c898e0ec5fbee83700da5b982b1c9539f1e1b34f  .uv-pkg/bin/uv' \
#          'f7f964cfa9da23653baec6e6f7750719977ad944ed9f95fe52fe3a620506891d  models/VoxCPM2/model.safetensors' \
#          '94b5d51e107e0507d4acc976cfdadb64edd6fd06d1f751dadbf2fd1594274bf1  models/VoxCPM2/audiovae.pth' | sha256sum -c
#   3. Python env: uv-managed Python 3.11, which uv finds in ~/.local/share/uv/python from step 2.
#      Not the system 3.11: torch.compile needs Python.h, which the system 3.11 lacks. Exact versions
#      come from Helios:
#        cd ~/voxcpm && ~/.uv-pkg/bin/uv venv --python-preference only-managed --python 3.11 .venv
#        VIRTUAL_ENV=$PWD/.venv ~/.uv-pkg/bin/uv pip install --index-strategy unsafe-best-match \
#          --extra-index-url https://download.pytorch.org/whl/cu128 -r requirements-voxcpm.txt
#   4. Let the service user read these files and nothing else. These are per-user ACLs, not
#      world-readable files (`sudo apt install acl` if setfacl is missing):
#        setfacl -R -m u:voxcpm:rX,d:u:voxcpm:rX ~/voxcpm ~/tts-voices ~/.local/share/uv/python ~/models/VoxCPM2
#   5. Pick the card. CUDA takes a UUID, so the choice survives slot or PCI-order changes:
#        echo 'CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES=GPU-<the 3090 UUID from step 0>' | sudo tee /etc/voxcpm-tts.env
#   6. sudo cp /tmp/voxcpm-tts-saturn.service /etc/systemd/system/voxcpm-tts.service
#      sudo systemctl daemon-reload && sudo systemctl enable --now voxcpm-tts
#      The first start compiles kernels (torch.compile, a few minutes). They persist in
#      /var/cache/voxcpm, so later starts take ~15-30 s. Watch: journalctl -u voxcpm-tts -f
#      If it fails with "Permission denied" under /home/labadmin, re-run step 4. If anything inside
#      needs loopback (not expected), the journal shows it as a refused connection to 127.0.0.1.
#   7. Checks:
#      - The voxcpm python process (~7.4 GiB) is the only process on the 3090:
#        nvidia-smi --query-compute-apps=gpu_name,pid,process_name,used_memory --format=csv
#      - Speed + memory tests (docs/VOICE_AND_TTS.md → VoxCPM2 on Saturn, step 2): texts of several
#        lengths up to the 2000-char maximum, with and without a style note, all succeed in under
#        ~45 s (the orchestrator gives up at 60 s), the journal shows no CUDA out-of-memory error,
#        and the 3090 shows at most 14 GiB used (`nvidia-smi --query-gpu=name,memory.used
#        --format=csv`; TTSGPUMemoryLow fires above 16). Write down the used memory. It can still
#        creep with time (on Helios it grew 7.3 -> 9.3 GiB on short texts), so check the 7-day
#        peak a week after cutover (query in the runbook).
#      - From Jupiter: curl -s http://10.0.0.58:8004/health returns ok with the voice listed.
#      - From Jupiter over the tailnet, which is NOT allowed: curl -m 3 http://saturn-3090.tail74fc4a.ts.net:8004/health
#        must fail. From any LAN device other than Jupiter (a laptop): the same check against
#        10.0.0.58 must fail.
#      - On Saturn: `journalctl -u voxcpm-tts | grep -i firewall` prints nothing. systemd starts
#        WITHOUT the IP filter, and says so there, if BPF firewalling is unavailable.
#        `systemctl show -p IPAddressAllow,IPAddressDeny voxcpm-tts` shows the two lines below.
#      - Speed and memory comparison vs Helios: docs/VOICE_AND_TTS.md → VoxCPM2 on Saturn.
#      Logs: this journal is NOT in Loki or the self-audit (Saturn runs no promtail).
#
# Weights: openbmb/VoxCPM2 (Apache-2.0), HF revision 32279effe8c19989596f05d353d1447f51d9e915, at
# /home/labadmin/models/VoxCPM2, loaded offline (sha256s in step 2).
# Logs: journalctl -u voxcpm-tts (Saturn journals are not shipped to Loki). Text is never logged.

[Unit]
Description=VoxCPM2 TTS Server for Brain Gateway (Saturn)
After=network-online.target nvidia-persistenced.service
Wants=network-online.target

[Service]
Type=simple
Restart=on-failure
RestartSec=10
# Load ~15 s from a warm page cache; the first start also runs torch.compile.
TimeoutStartSec=600

# Card choice lives in /etc/voxcpm-tts.env (CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES=GPU-<uuid>, step 5). No `-` prefix:
# a missing file must fail the start, not let CUDA pick whichever card comes first.
EnvironmentFile=/etc/voxcpm-tts.env
Environment="CUDA_DEVICE_ORDER=PCI_BUS_ID"
Environment="HF_HUB_OFFLINE=1"
Environment="VOXCPM_MODEL=/home/labadmin/models/VoxCPM2"
Environment="VOXCPM_VOICES_DIR=/home/labadmin/tts-voices"
Environment="VOXCPM_PORT=8004"
# LAN address only, so nothing listens on Saturn's tailnet address. Needs Saturn's DHCP reservation
# for 10.0.0.58 on the router (TTS_URL depends on it too).
Environment="VOXCPM_HOST=10.0.0.58"
Environment="VOXCPM_OUTPUT_SR=24000"
# Writable state (torch.compile / triton kernels, any library home-dir cache) lives in
# /var/cache/voxcpm; everything under /home is a read-only bind.
CacheDirectory=voxcpm
Environment="HOME=/var/cache/voxcpm"
Environment="TORCHINDUCTOR_CACHE_DIR=/var/cache/voxcpm/inductor"
Environment="TRITON_CACHE_DIR=/var/cache/voxcpm/triton"
Environment="XDG_CACHE_HOME=/var/cache/voxcpm"
# tqdm progress bars per synthesis turn the journal into "[blob data]".
Environment="TQDM_DISABLE=1"
# Peak RSS ~10.4 GiB during load + compile (Saturn has 62 GiB).
MemoryMax=16G

# Sandbox: as the Helios unit, with its own nologin user. ProtectHome=tmpfs hides the rest of /home,
# including the Jess backups Saturn holds (/home/labadmin/*-backups).
User=voxcpm
Group=voxcpm
NoNewPrivileges=yes
ProtectSystem=strict
ProtectHome=tmpfs
BindReadOnlyPaths=/home/labadmin/voxcpm /home/labadmin/.local/share/uv/python /home/labadmin/tts-voices /home/labadmin/models/VoxCPM2
PrivateTmp=yes
ProtectProc=invisible
# Saturn is also the backup target and an experiment box: keep its data mounts out of view.
InaccessiblePaths=-/mnt -/media -/srv
DevicePolicy=closed
# CUDA opens every /dev/nvidiaN while enumerating, even with one card visible; without the node
# torch silently falls back to CPU. CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES (/etc/voxcpm-tts.env) limits it to the voice card.
DeviceAllow=/dev/nvidia0 rw
DeviceAllow=/dev/nvidia1 rw
DeviceAllow=/dev/nvidiactl rw
DeviceAllow=/dev/nvidia-uvm rw
DeviceAllow=/dev/nvidia-uvm-tools rw
ProtectKernelTunables=yes
ProtectKernelModules=yes
ProtectKernelLogs=yes
ProtectControlGroups=yes
ProtectClock=yes
ProtectHostname=yes
RestrictNamespaces=yes
RestrictRealtime=yes
RestrictSUIDSGID=yes
LockPersonality=yes
CapabilityBoundingSet=
AmbientCapabilities=
RestrictAddressFamilies=AF_INET AF_INET6 AF_UNIX
SystemCallArchitectures=native
UMask=0077
# Host-firewall substitute: Jupiter only. Jupiter's containers (the orchestrator, the blackbox probe)
# reach the LAN through Jupiter's own address. No loopback either: nothing on Saturn needs to call
# it, and loopback would admit anything tailscaled or a local service proxies, plus DNS through the
# resolver stub. This is tighter than the Helios unit on purpose: Saturn is on the tailnet, and this
# server is unauthenticated and speaks in the cloned voice. NEVER add Helios or the LAN here. The
# Wyoming bridge (tts/wyoming_jessica_bridge.py, :10301, no auth) would then turn any LAN host into
# a voice relay. Do NOT add MemoryDenyWriteExecute or PrivateDevices: both break CUDA.
IPAddressDeny=any
IPAddressAllow=10.0.0.248/32

WorkingDirectory=/home/labadmin/voxcpm
ExecStart=/home/labadmin/voxcpm/.venv/bin/python /home/labadmin/voxcpm/voxcpm_server.py

StandardOutput=journal
StandardError=journal
SyslogIdentifier=voxcpm-tts

[Install]
WantedBy=multi-user.target
